          %h2#idea Idea
          %p#IsCalled
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            is called
            %em compactly generated
            – also called a “k-space”
            %sup#fnref:1
              %a{:href => "#fn:1", :rel => "footnote"} 1
            (
            %a{:href => "#Gale50"}> Gale 1950, 1.
            , following lectures by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witold+Hurewicz"} Hurewicz
            in 1948), “Kelley space” (
            %a{:href => "#GabrielZisman67"}> Gabriel &amp; Zisman 1967, III.4
            ), or “kaonic space” (
            %a{:href => "#Postnikov82"}> Postnikov 1982, p. 34
            ) – if its topology is detected by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+map"} continuous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/images"} images
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Hausdorff+spaces"} compact Hausdorff spaces
            inside it.
          %p
            As opposed to general topological spaces, compactly generated spaces form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+closed+category"} cartesian closed category
            while still being general enough for most purposes of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+topology"} general topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convenient+category+of+topological+spaces"} convenient category of topological spaces
            (
            %a{:href => "#Steenrod67"}> Steenrod 1967
            ) and as such have come to be commonly used in the foundations of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+topology"} algebraic topology
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            %em weakly Hausdorff
            spaces, due to
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#McCord69"} McCord 1969, Sec. 2
          %h2#Definition Definition
          %p
          #kContinuousFunction.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                \-continuous functions)
              %br/
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function"} function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
